924be332d0694d196f8dfc45f65759cb2adfbad240fdd72e05fa3d8c63684b9a,fairbridge1961,sea_level_indicator,Greymouth,narrative_fallback_2,,,inferred_map,,-6.0,MSL,reported,marine_terrace,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-8.5, -3.5]",null,other,"marine terrace at Greymouth, reported around 6 m depth",Greymouth,"forms and raised beaches. A eustatic factor was calculated, that differed with every observer. A new approach is now possible. The p",https://doi.org/10.1016/0079-1946(61)90004-0,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": null, ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""so that the top must have approximated 7 m below present sea level. A Morocco date marks the transgression above present sea level at 5970 4- 130 B.P. (L-398B: BROECKER,personal communication). A thalassostatic fluvial terrace at Pinjarra, Western Australia, contains wood 6 m above sea level that is dated 5680 4- 120 B.P. (Y-327; DEEVEYet al., 1959). In Borneo (Butir, in Brunei) the transgression is marked by shell beds dated at 5400 4200 B.P. (courtesy of G. E. WILFORDand W. S. BROECKER). A British date gives a sea level at zero once more at Tealham Moor (Somerset) at 5412 4- 130 B.P. (GODWIN et al., 1958); another date from mangrove wood, at present between tide-marks where it originally grew, is also a good indication of a zero sea leve! at this time; it comes from Takoradi Harbour (Gh""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 6.0, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, "