f37d09953de5bfc4556efb09ef1081757f6b3b7f2bc098160f11585ff6cf390b,peterson2016,sea_level_indicator,5.7. Projected increases in basin accommodation space and littoral sand sinks following future sea level rise,narrative_fallback_1,38.8950982,-77.0363849,inferred_text,10000.0,-7.5,MSL,reported,submerged_beach,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-10.0, -5.0]",5.0,stratigraphic_context,"submerged beach at 5.7. Projected increases in basin accommodation space and littoral sand sinks following future sea level rise, reported around 7.5 m depth","Washington, District of Columbia, United States","Accommodation space controls on incised-valley sediment accumulation rates during the Holocene marine transgression (0–11 ka) in Grays Harbor, a large meso-tidal estuary, Washington, USA",https://doi.org/10.1016/j.margeo.2016.06.012,0.95,"Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review. Geocoded from contextual query 'Washington, USA'.","{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": ""5.7. Projected increases in basin accommodation space and littoral sand sinks following future sea level rise"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""the predicted littoral sand sink in the Grays Harbor basin could be filled by littoral sand that currently exists in adjacent barrier beaches. If all of the littoral sand that fills the estuary sand sink is supplied by shoreline retreat of the adjacent barriers (20 km in length), and assuming average vertical beach profile depths of 5\u201310 m, then net shoreward erosion distances of 500\u20131000 m of the current shoreline could occur in the study area from a potential 1.0 m submergence of the Grays Harbor basin.""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 7.5, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived