cbb6193c77a3ac78c563c855f16f5199cfc340aac935a7a6f795d35c973733bf,walis_v1_0_post_review,sea_level_indicator,Puerto Williams Corrales Viejos - Corrales Viejos,RSL_288@row2751,-54.935665,-67.465619,reported,0.0151699301222851,11.08,MSL,reported,beachrock,Beach deposit or beachrock,"[-1.52, 1.2599999999999998]",135.0,luminescence,"Beach deposit or beachrock; 0.9-1.2 m thick beach deposit with marine shell fragments. This is underlain by terrestrial sediments, and overlain by a thin layer of terrestrial sediments and Wisconsin aged glacial till from the Moat Glaciation. There was no evidence of glacio-tectonic movements, so this was interpreted to be in-situ. The shells were fragmented, but showed little abrasion, so it was interpreted that they had not undergone much transport. Fauna assemblages indicate that the environment was similar to present. Bjork et al (2021) revisited the site and collected IRSL samples.","Puerto Williams Corrales Viejos, Corrales Viejos, Magallanes y de la Antartica Chilena, Chile","Rabassa et al., 2008 Bjork et al., 2021 Gordillo and Isla, 2011 Gordillo et al 2010 Gowan et al., 2021",https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5979520,0.98,"WALIS quality RSL=3, age=3. Originally accepted=Yes; accepted by other study=blank. Elevation source: Paleo RSL (m). WALIS reported age. Originally reported ID: 15101. Analysis ID: BJ21-001-001. WALIS LUM ID: 303. Record Created by: Evan Gowan. Last Update: 2020-12-09 18:35:22.","{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": ""WALIS Summary.csv"", ""section"": ""RSL_288@row2751"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""WALIS row RSL_288: Sea Level Indicator; Beach deposit or beachrock; elevation 10.95 m; paleo RSL 11.08 m; dating Luminescence.""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": 10.95, ""reported_depth_m"": null, ""reported_datum"": ""Mean Sea Level / General definition"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": 2.07}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": 11.08, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""der