ab141fbe12110f1a5afb41020475f9fbc4ef8529de0354e01f604afd29ac0f84,walis_v1_0_post_review,sea_level_indicator,Minchin Hole Cave Inner Beach - Inner Beach,RSL_4005@row3418,51.5621,-4.085623,reported,0.0,9.34,MSL,reported,beachrock,Beach deposit or beachrock,"[-1.4049999999999998, 3.905]","[71.0, 130.0]",aar,"Beach deposit or beachrock; The Inner Beach is the lower most deposit within the Minchin Hole Cave sequence. The Inner Beach is two metres thick of bedded sand with marine shells sitting on top of the limestone basement, indicative of an intertidal beach deposit. The upper contact of the Inner Beach is erosive and lies at 11.6 m OD. On top of this unit is 'Lower Red Cave Earth' which is a thick deposit of poorly sorted angular limestone clasts within a red clayey matrix and lacking stratification, indicative of or equivalent to regional periglacial 'Head' deposits. overlying the Head deposits is the Patella Beach occupying a vertical range of 6.2 to 12.4 m OD with varied particle size (from shingle to boulders) and indicative of a storm beach deposit.","Minchin Hole Cave Inner Beach, Inner Beach, Vale of Glamorgan, Great Britain (UK)","Sutcliffe et al., 1987 Davies 1983 Hollin et al., 1993 Bowen et al., 1985",https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5979520,0.93,"WALIS quality RSL=2, age=2. Originally accepted=Yes; accepted by other study=blank. Elevation source: Paleo RSL (m). WALIS stratigraphic upper/lower age bounds. Originally reported ID: AAL4603. Analysis ID: BO85-001-001. WALIS AAR ID: 465. Record Created by: Rob Barnett. Last Update: 2021-05-28 12:04:52.","{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": ""WALIS Summary.csv"", ""section"": ""RSL_4005@row3418"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""WALIS row RSL_4005: Sea Level Indicator; Beach deposit or beachrock; elevation 10.6 m; paleo RSL 9.34 m; dating AAR.""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": 10.6, ""reported_depth_m"": null, ""reported_datum"": ""OD (Ordnance Datum Newlyn)"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": 2.83}","{""raw_raster_v