7f5536539e9dc98812f19c0b346842f01d5a8572b07bc53a65df010291c24a89,walis_v1_0_post_review,sea_level_indicator,Taieri Beach upthrown (east) side Akatore Fault - upthrown (east) side Akatore Fault,RSL_47@row5216,-46.074055,170.2008,reported,0.0006938154664871958,5.59,unknown,reported,beachrock,Beach deposit or beachrock,"[-2.19, 1.81]",71.0,luminescence,"Beach deposit or beachrock; The raised beach deposit (identified by Bishop (1994) as 'h2') at 8-11 m above high sea level consists of two sand units upon a wave-cut platform capped by a loess. The lowest sand unit (sample NZ10), c. 1 m thick, is coarse, well-sorted reddish-orange sand and gravel overlying the wave-cut surface formed upon schist basement. This unit is overlain by c. 1.5 m fine, lightly weathered yellow sands (sample NZ9) and capped by c. 1 m of loess (sample NZ8). The coarse and fine sands are considered a raised beach sequence. The loess reaches 3-4 m thickness in places and was estimated by Bishop (1994) to be <30 ka. Elevation and Paleo SL in WALIS determined using the lowest, coarse sand, unit. This site was revised by Litchfield and Lian 2004. This site is on the upthrown side of the Akatore Fault, which resumed activity within the Holocene.","Taieri Beach upthrown (east) side Akatore Fault, upthrown (east) side Akatore Fault, Otago, New Zealand (Aotearoa)","Rees-Jones et al 2000 Barrell et al 1998 Bishop 1994 Litchfield and Lian, 2004 Ryan et al., 2020",https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5979520,0.905,"WALIS quality RSL=2, age=1. Originally accepted=Yes; accepted by other study=blank. Elevation source: Paleo RSL (m). WALIS reported age. Originally reported ID: NZ10. Analysis ID: RE00-001-001. WALIS LUM ID: 22. Record Created by: Deirdre Ryan. Last Update: 2020-04-17 04:58:57.","{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": ""WALIS Summary.csv"", ""section"": ""RSL_47@row5216"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""WALIS row RSL_47: Sea Level Indicator; Beach deposit or beachrock; elevation 5.4 m; paleo RSL 5.59 