c88776337b92eade479a70c25ee7225e89c0eff16d9ac3f4b6184582194987b9,walis_v1_0_post_review,sea_level_indicator,Taieri Beach,RSL_48@row5218,-46.074055,170.200809,reported,0.0,5.85,MSL,reported,beachrock,Beach deposit or beachrock,"[-1.4649999999999999, 0.765]",117.0,luminescence,"Beach deposit or beachrock; Beach sands are identified within a marine cliff section: c. 5 m of unconsolidated sediment resting on shore platform c. 4 m above high sea level formed upon schist basement. The sediments consist of two quartz-rich beach sand units, together c. 3 m thick, that are overlain by a single sheet of loess c. 1 m thick. The sediments have previously been described and dated by Rees-Jones et al. (2000). In this work the lower sand unit was sampled c. 1 m above the shore platform. It is expected that the sediment age would closely date the time of terrace formation. The source for map coordinates provided in each publication differ and result in two different lat/long for the publications. This site is on the upthrown side of the Akatore Fault, which resumed activity within the Holocene.","Taieri Beach, Otago, New Zealand (Aotearoa)","Litchfield and Lian, 2004 Barrell et al 1998 Rees-Jones et al 2000 Ryan et al., 2020",https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5979520,0.98,"WALIS quality RSL=2, age=4. Originally accepted=Yes; accepted by other study=blank. Elevation source: Paleo RSL (m). WALIS reported age. Originally reported ID: W2857. Analysis ID: LI04-001-001. WALIS LUM ID: 24. Record Created by: Deirdre Ryan. Last Update: 2020-06-01 19:06:53.","{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": ""WALIS Summary.csv"", ""section"": ""RSL_48@row5218"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""WALIS row RSL_48: Sea Level Indicator; Beach deposit or beachrock; elevation 5.5 m; paleo RSL 5.85 m; dating Luminescence.""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": 5.5, ""reported_depth_m"": null, ""reported_datum"": ""Mean Sea Level / General definition"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": 2.73}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null