4f8a72b482ead34b43eb3365c31d60ed06d5ca65c53e19e6c9714aa7c4ff9f50,beaman2008,sea_level_indicator,4.3.1. Pre-Last Glacial Maximum,narrative_fallback_4,-22.4318616,149.675892,inferred_text,5000.0,-96.0,MSL,reported,marine_terrace,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-98.5, -93.5]",500.0,stratigraphic_context,"marine terrace at 4.3.1. Pre-Last Glacial Maximum, reported around 96 m depth","Great Barrier Reef Coast Marine Park - Styx River, Isaac Regional, Queensland, Australia","New evidence for drowned shelf edge reefs in the Great Barrier Reef, Australia",https://doi.org/10.1016/j.margeo.2007.08.001,0.95,"Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review. Geocoded from contextual query 'Great Barrier Reef, Queensland, Australia'.","{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": ""4.3.1. Pre-Last Glacial Maximum"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""ka) and is characterised by 5 major glacial/inter-glacial cycles and associated eustatic sea-level and climate variations (Imbrie et al., 1984; Lambeck and Chappell, 2001; Lea et al., 2002). Fig. 11A shows a plot of the terrace and step depths of between 78 and 114 m against the 500 ka sea-level curve. We note that the sea-level has cycled through the 78 to 114 m depth range for about 90 ka or about 19% of the pre-LGM period. Within this period, the terraces may represent stepped constructional or erosional surfaces that were formed within this depth range over any of the lower sea-level periods and are now preserved at the shelf edge. However, lithologic and age data from these surfaces will be required to confirm the origin and evolution of the terrace and step features.""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 96.0, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": n