c710b2e58c1ae8eaaa519b0d8e1902474da3f0c6f442cc1b240a12b0f0335bdd,clague1973,sea_level_indicator,clague1973,heuristic_feature_1,5.44,5.46,reported,100.0,7.0,MSL,SRTM15+V2,guyot_or_drowned_platform,heuristically extracted from explicit text,null,null,other,"AGE OF KōKO SEAMOUNT, EMPEROR SEAMOUNT CHAIN",clague1973,"AGE OF KōKO SEAMOUNT, EMPEROR SEAMOUNT CHAIN",,0.75,"Extracted using heuristic parser from pdf text after methods: mineru_hybrid_auto_markdown, mineru_content_list.","{""page"": ""1-2"", ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": null, ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""e dredges from K\u014dko Seamount produced numerous volcanic cobbles suitable for K\u2013Ar dating. # 2. Results K\u014dko Seamount is a large, broadly elliptical guyot about 200 km long and 110 km wide at its base that rises from a depth of about 4400 m to within 360 m of sea level. It is shown on earlier bathymetric charts [6] as two smaller seamounts, but more recent and detailed bathymetry demonstrates that the seamount is a single guyot [22, 23]. K\u014dko Seamount has the typical smooth form of Hawaiian volcanoes [3] and was probably produced by several coalescing shields. Samples from K\u014dko Seamount were""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": null, ""reported_datum"": null, ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": 7.0, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": 7.0, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Elevation sampled from SRTM15+V2 at authoritative coordinates because source elevation was not reported.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""No direct age model extracted from text.""}]",7,7,7,7
