47666a2b192b70053252e774ee224ce30fc36e601a6efe6f6afd8479e1f89cd5,gharibreza2006,sea_level_indicator,Narrative evidence locality,narrative_fallback_1,,,inferred_map,,-15.0,MSL,reported,submerged_beach,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-17.5, -12.5]",null,other,"submerged beach at Narrative evidence locality, reported around 15 m depth",Narrative evidence locality,"estigated marine terrace uplift rates. However, detailed investigation of the Quaternary history of the coastal DOI:10.2112/05A-0020.",https://doi.org/10.2112/05A-0020.1,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": null, ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""80) standpoint for a nonfaulted area (2.7 mm/y), it uplifted 12 m and reached 15 m elevations. Then, accumulations of sea-level rise and uplifting values resulted in the location of the B layer at 15 m above the present shoreline. In the other theory, it was suggested that at 4690 \u2afe 40 YBP, sea level had been located at about the present shoreline elevation. Accordingly, Chabahar Bay was uplifted at 3.2 mm/y rates. This result was greater than the rate of uplifting faulted areas, which was suggested by VITA-FINZI (1980).""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 15.0, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""Narrative fallback age context; review before analytical use.""}]",-15,-15,-15,-15
