7289307eefb02caffc4c57b3deeb1e91b521a3e3124ea367f105eb119c89e7a7,herzer1971,sea_level_indicator,Geologic History of the Summit Region,narrative_fallback_3,,,inferred_map,,-82.5,MSL,reported,marine_terrace,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-85.0, -80.0]",null,other,"marine terrace at Geologic History of the Summit Region, reported around 82.5 m depth",Geologic History of the Summit Region,"Bowie Seamount. A Recently Active, Flat-topped Seamount in the Northeast Pacific Ocean $^{1}$",,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": ""Geologic History of the Summit Region"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""The highest peak, standing 3100 m above the ocean floor and dominating the southwestern half of the seamount, has a relatively flat top (Fig. 2). The flat top actually comprises two terraces. The lower one forms a conspicuous platform 220 m to 250 m (approximately 120 to 140 fathoms) deep and is surmounted by a string of low hills. The second, shallower terrace level is apparent on the highest hills which are almost truncated at depths between 65 and 100 m (35 and 55 fathoms) (Fig. 3). Small rugged pinnacles project above this upper terrace level, the tallest coming to within 30 m of the ocean surface (Scrimger and Halliday 1971).""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 82.5, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""Narrative fallback age context; review before analytical use.""}]",-82.5,-82.5,-82.5,-82.5
