e3b64d7affee8ee40a3a627fff4bd58510597061b5a869260c501b0395f5059c,tamura2008,sea_level_indicator,Narrative evidence locality,narrative_fallback_1,,,inferred_map,,-2.5,MSL,reported,marine_terrace,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-5.0, 0.0]",6.0,stratigraphic_context,"marine terrace at Narrative evidence locality, reported around 2.5 m depth",Narrative evidence locality,doi:10.1016/j.margeo.2007.10.002,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.margeo.2007.10.002,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": null, ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""1/150 at a water depth of 0\u20135 m and 1/200 at 5\u201315 m depth. The landward margin of the Kujukuri stand plain is defined by a plateau and hills, 30\u2013120 m above sea level, the surfaces of which correspond to a raised marine terrace of marine oxygen isotope stage 5e age (Fig. 1B and C; Unozawa et al., 1983). The margin coincides with the shoreline at 6 ka (Moriwaki, 1979), prior to the shoreline progradation that resulted in the growth of this strand plain, which is 10 km wide at maximum. Seven rivers run through the strand plain, but their contribution to sediment supply of the coast has been relatively small. The rapid retreat of coastal cliffs at Byobugaura and Taitosaki, the seaward edges of the plateau and hills surrounding the Kujukuri plain (Fig. 1B), has created a large sediment supply""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 2.5, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""stratigraphic_context"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": 6.0, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""Narrative fallback age context; review before analytical use.""}]",-2.5,-2.5,-2.5,-2.5
