f187cf444cc9a39907ad2633e9827881070d333b3a727ed28a7e2041326d47d3,vardar2014,sea_level_indicator,Narrative evidence locality,narrative_fallback_1,,,inferred_map,,-3.0,MSL,reported,submerged_beach,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-5.5, -0.5]",null,other,"submerged beach at Narrative evidence locality, reported around 3 m depth",Narrative evidence locality,ORIGINAL RESEARCH PAPER,https://doi.org/10.1007/s11001-013-9210-8,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": null, ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""also called a transparent Unit 1 mud drape (Aksu et al. 1999), Unit 3-D1 (Yalt\u0131rak and Alpar 2002a) and Unit 3 (Kus\u0327c\u0327u et al. 2009; Fig. 5c). Two large paleolakes in Gemlik and Band\u0131rma Bays can be traced from the paleoshorelines at -60.5 m without -3.3 m GIA (Figs 7c, 8a\u2013d), -50.3 m without -3.3 m GIA (Fig. 7a, b) and from the multibeam bathymetry where the shorelines are fault controlled (Fig. 6d). This setting is similar to the present topographic conditions observed between the Sea of Marmara and the shallow freshwater lakes in the southern Marmara region, which are depositional areas on the courses of main rivers. With an elevation of 15 m above the present mean sea level, Lake Manyas reaches a depth of 3 m. On the other hand, the water level of Lake Uluabat is ?1 m above sea level,""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 3.0, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""Narrative fallback age co