7ae29783672ac20476dd6091fcf3c6914508f617e9cc7cdd3165ad53cb3153f9,yen2021,sea_level_indicator,Narrative evidence locality,narrative_fallback_1,,,inferred_map,,-0.0,MSL,reported,submerged_beach,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-2.5, 2.5]",2.5,stratigraphic_context,"submerged beach at Narrative evidence locality, reported around 0 m depth",Narrative evidence locality,Late Pleistocene-Holocene sedimentary evolution in the coastal zone of the Red River Delta,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2020.e05872,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": null, ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""marshy mud \ufb02ats along the tidal coast. - Modern sand bar facies: The modern sand bars of the Red River Delta include the Den, Vanh, Lu, Ngan, Thoi, and Mo Sand bars. These sand bar systems mark the 5th generation of the shoreline since the last ~2.5 Ka. compared to the previous ancient sand bars. It is demonstrated by a high concentration of quartz (Q reaches over 90%), very poor sortness (So: 1.2\u20131.3), and good roundness (Ro: 0.5\u20130.9). - Coastal shallow water sandy mud facies: The coastal shallow water sandy mud facies are distributed from a depth of 0m\u201315m, creating a submarine, \ufb02at, fan-shaped plain, tilting seaward at an angle of ~0.51o. Characteristics of the sediment properties are poor sortness (So: 2.3\u20132.7), relatively low sand/mud ratio (1/9 - 4/6), and quite high acid-alkaline in""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 0.0, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""stratigraphic_context"", ""relation"": ""unknown""