fe31413c29085f3e500066480cb3e68bf55a667287be14e12b231ff4dd6b1ff3,grant1980,sea_level_indicator,Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines,narrative_fallback_1,,,inferred_map,,-70.0,MSL,reported,marine_terrace,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-72.5, -67.5]",null,other,"marine terrace at Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines, reported around 70 m depth",Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines,Quaternary Sea-Level Change in Atlantic Canada as an Indication of Crustal Delevelling,,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": ""Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""level, by considering the age and depth of certain submerged shore marks. Kranck (1972) drew attention to five drowned levels of terrace formation to depths of 60\u201380 m in Northumberland Strait and correlated the lowest with the Late Wisconsinan dated marine limit at +23 m on western Prince Edward Island. As the lower one or two terraces lie beyond the inferred limit of latest glaciers (Grant, 1976a) either could represent the stadial eustatic lowered sea level, and hence predate somewhat the emerged shorelines. Similarly, Loring and Nota (1973, p. 46) describe a broad terrace encircling the Magdalen Islands at -62 to -70 m. If it marks the last stadial sea level there, its greater depth is consistent""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 70.0, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""Narrative fallback age context; review before analytical use.""}]",-70,-70,-70,-70
7333bce3237375b3504f6741553afd3d0f03ab4487ca51316babd43b87ce6902,grant1980,sea_level_indicator,Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines,narrative_fallback_2,,,inferred_map,,-112.5,MSL,reported,marine_terrace,narrative fallback: submerged geomorphic sea-level feature,"[-115.0, -110.0]",null,other,"marine terrace at Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines, reported around 112.5 m depth",Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines,Quaternary Sea-Level Change in Atlantic Canada as an Indication of Crustal Delevelling,,0.55,Narrative fallback cluster from prose description of submerged palaeolandscape; depth uncertainty widened pending human review.,"{""page"": null, ""figure"": null, ""table"": null, ""section"": ""Late Wisconsinan Emerged and Submerged Shorelines"", ""quote_or_paraphrase"": ""a) but its proximity (within 50 km) to raised shorelines is problematical. The same contrast between a deeper terrain with irregular till-like surface and a shallower smooth surface, locally with an associated erosional terrace, was found at depths of 105\u2013120 m on the Scotian Shelf (Fig. 4), and taken to mark the late-glacial sea level position (King, 1970). In the Bay of Fundy, the same contact shallows to less than 37 m (Fader, King and Machean, 1977). Iceberg scouring is now recognized as the cause of the irregular topography, but the depth remains a measure of postglacial transgression (King, 1976). Finally, M\u00fcller and Milliman (1973) reported barnacle\u2013mollusc sediments at 85\u2013100 m on the Grand Banks (Fig. 4) that dated 17,100 \u00b1 270 y and 19,000 \u00b1 330 y, and attributed them to a \u2018W\u00fcrm""}","{""reported_elevation_m"": null, ""reported_depth_m"": 112.5, ""reported_datum"": ""MSL"", ""reported_uncertainty_m"": null}","{""raw_raster_value_m"": null, ""applied_correction_m"": null, ""derived_elevation_m"": null, ""derived_depth_m"": null, ""derivation_notes"": ""Existing elevation preserved; no raster overwrite applied.""}","[{""method"": ""other"", ""relation"": ""unknown"", ""age_ka"": null, ""uncertainty_ka"": null, ""material"": null, ""notes"": ""Narrative fallback age context; review before analytical use.""}]",-112.5,-112.5,-112.5,-112.5
